# Passkeys frontend TODO

Generated by the light-it auth package: the passkey services
(`src/services/auth/passkeys/*`), the `/account/passkeys` page
(`src/routes/_private/account/passkeys/**`), where a signed-in user adds a
passkey, sees each one's name and creation date, renames it and deletes it,
and a "Sign in with passkey" button for the login form
(`src/routes/(public)/_guest/login/-components/passkey-login-button.tsx` and
`-hooks/use-sign-in-with-passkey.ts`). Delete this file once you've read it.

The TanStack Router plugin adds the route to `src/routeTree.gen.ts` the next time
`vite` runs (`dev` or `build`). Type-check after that.

## How it works

1. Every call uses the cookie session through the shared `api` from
   `@/config/api`; there is no token.
2. Add: the password goes to `POST passkeys/registration-options`, which returns the
   WebAuthn creation options. `startRegistration` from `@simplewebauthn/browser`
   (base64url JSON to the `ArrayBuffer`s the WebAuthn API wants and back, no
   dependencies of its own) runs `navigator.credentials.create`, and the result
   goes with the name to `POST passkeys`. The backend spends the challenge on that
   call, so a retry starts over from the password.
3. A wrong password is a `422` on the `password` field and shows under it.
4. A cancelled or timed-out browser prompt, a device that can't create passkeys, a
   device that already holds a passkey for the account (the browser's
   `InvalidStateError` or the backend's `409`) and an expired challenge (`410`) get
   their own message; any other failure shows `common.requestError`.
5. Rename and delete refresh the list. Delete asks for the password again
   (`POST passkeys/{id}/delete`).
6. Without WebAuthn support in the browser the page says so instead of offering
   "Add passkey".

## Optional: link the page from the sidebar

The package does not edit your navigation. To add a link, in
`src/routes/_private/-components/sidebar/sidebar.tsx` add this entry at the end of
the `links` array:

```tsx
{ path: "/account/passkeys", label: t("navigation.links.passkeys"), icon: <Icons.Lock /> },
```

Its label is the short `navigation.links.passkeys` key from the i18n step below,
like the other links. `Lock` is the closest icon the template's `Icons` set ships;
to use another one, add it to `src/components/ui/icons/available-icons.ts` first.

## How sign-in works

1. `authenticateWithPasskey` calls `ensureCsrf()`, then `POST auth/passkeys/login-options`
   for an anonymous, single-use challenge and its `ceremony_id`. The options carry no
   `allowCredentials`, so `startAuthentication` lets the user pick any passkey the
   device holds for the site. The result goes with the `ceremony_id` to
   `POST auth/passkeys/login`.
2. A normal sign-in creates the cookie session and returns the user.
   `useSignInWithPasskey` fetches the current-user query again, like the template's
   `useLogin`, and the button navigates to the `redirect` search param (or `/`)
   through `redirectTarget`, like the login form.
3. When the user has 2FA, the backend answers with the same challenge as the password
   login. If the 2FA frontend was already generated when this file was, the hook puts it
   in the 2FA challenge store and navigates to `/two-factor` or `/two-factor/setup`,
   keeping `redirect`, so a passkey never skips the second factor. Otherwise the hook
   only knows the user response, and a challenge shows `common.requestError`. If you
   add 2FA later, delete `-hooks/use-sign-in-with-passkey.ts` and run `auth:setup`
   with Passkeys again: it writes the 2FA-aware hook and skips every other file.
4. A cancelled or timed-out prompt, a browser without WebAuthn, a passkey the backend
   doesn't know (`422` `passkey_not_recognised`, e.g. deleted from the account) and an
   expired challenge (`410`) get their own toast; anything else shows
   `common.requestError`.

## Manual step: add the button to the login form

The package never edits your files. In
`src/routes/(public)/_guest/login/-components/login-form.tsx`:

1. Add this import as the last import of the file:

   ```ts
   import { PasskeyLoginButton } from "./passkey-login-button";
   ```

2. Add the button on the line after the submit button's closing `</Button>` (the
   one showing `{t("login.login")}`):

   ```tsx
   <PasskeyLoginButton />
   ```

The button is `type="button"`, so it never submits the password form. This step
touches neither the `useLogin` import nor the `loginMutation` line, so it applies
the same way before or after the 2FA login-hook step.

## i18n keys

`src/i18n/locales/en.json` is not edited for you. Add this block at the top level:

```json
"passkeys": {
  "title": "Passkeys",
  "description": "Passkeys let you sign in with your fingerprint, face or screen lock instead of a password.",
  "loading": "Loading your passkeys...",
  "loadError": "We couldn't load your passkeys. Reload the page to retry.",
  "empty": "You haven't added a passkey yet.",
  "createdAt": "Added {date}",
  "signIn": {
    "submit": "Sign in with passkey"
  },
  "form": {
    "name": "Passkey name",
    "nameTooLong": "Use 100 characters or fewer."
  },
  "add": {
    "open": "Add passkey",
    "title": "Add a passkey",
    "description": "Name it after the device it lives on, then confirm with your password.",
    "submit": "Continue",
    "success": "Passkey added"
  },
  "rename": {
    "open": "Rename",
    "title": "Rename passkey",
    "description": "Pick a name that tells you which device this passkey is on.",
    "submit": "Save",
    "success": "Passkey renamed"
  },
  "delete": {
    "open": "Delete",
    "title": "Delete passkey",
    "description": "Enter your password to delete {name}. You won't be able to use it again.",
    "submit": "Delete",
    "success": "{name} was deleted"
  },
  "errors": {
    "cancelled": "The passkey request was cancelled or timed out.",
    "unsupported": "This browser or device can't create passkeys.",
    "alreadyRegistered": "This device already has a passkey for your account.",
    "challengeExpired": "That took too long. Try again.",
    "notRecognised": "This passkey isn't registered. Sign in with your password and add it again."
  }
}
```

Only if you add the sidebar link above, add `passkeys` next to the existing
`navigation.links` keys:

```json
"navigation": { "links": { "passkeys": "Passkeys" } }
```

The screens also reuse `form.password`, `form.errors.required`, `buttons.cancel`,
`login.success` and `common.requestError`, which the boilerplate already has.

